{
	title: 'EVAL runs JavaScript in a child process and chains tools',
	spec: 'The built-in tool EVAL runs JavaScript or TypeScript in a child Bun process, one per call, so its code never blocks the host event loop: only an explicit host() call runs in the host thread. Its code is literal text after the header, running to the end of the cmd: no quoting or escaping, and command splitting never splits it (task 0nt). One whole string literal or { code } is also accepted. A single expression returns its value; otherwise the code is an async function body that may return. await tool("<call text>") asks the host to run any Hal tool with its own checks and returns its text output; a call that would need approval is refused with that reason; a failed call throws. Each request reaches the host as its own IPC message, and the child keeps at most 64 requests in flight. await host(fn, ...args) runs fn source in the host with ctx and tool for live state; fn sees only its arguments, and a value that cannot be cloned returns as inspected text; HELP says it blocks Hal while synchronous, so keep it short. Only the returned value and console.* output enter context, so scripts can chain tools and MCP calls (Code Mode) and filter large output. ctx gives cwd, sessionId and callId. The child runs in its own process group, and aborting a running EVAL (Esc, STOP t<n>) kills the group and its pending tool calls. Timers outlive the call in the child, which then is a background job, as for BASH: STOP, /kill, closing its tab and Hal exiting kill its group; a call without a recorded card has no name to stop, so its group dies when it returns. A timer whose callback throws stops and reports a session notice. The child exits when the host disconnects; a child that dies early fails the call with its exit code and stderr. EVAL is effectful and runs without approval: agents can do the same through BASH, so gating it would be fake security.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Incident 2026-11-22: a remote EVAL ran 32 lanes of await tool() on a plugin tool plus a 27 ms sync SQLite write each. Its plugin was deleted mid-run; tool() then rejected without a macrotask, so the lanes retried and wrote without yielding, and the host froze for ~6 min (no diag lines, web unreachable). Guidance (keep sync under 100 ms) cannot prevent this. Invariant restored: EVAL code never blocks the host event loop. Measured locally (12-too): child startup ~7 ms vs Worker ~2 ms, but Worker.terminate() waited 909 ms for a busy loop while SIGKILL took 1 ms, so a child process.',
		'Invariant (2026-11-22, 12-too): no EVAL process outlives what can stop it. Before, a child kept alive by timers dropped its abort listener on return, so nothing but a host disconnect ended it, and STOP refused a running EVAL; processes the code spawned survived SIGKILL of the child.',
		'Invariant: a scheduled or autoclose session whose EVAL child outlives its turn still closes its tab. The lingering child was a running job when the turn ended, so autoclose.finished refused and nothing rechecked; its exit now rechecks autoclose. Found in session 58-fan on 2026-11-27: 51 hourly models.dev tabs had piled up since Nov 25.'
	],
	needs: ['3fv', 'ep8']
}
